This project contains the jdaemon, which implements the
system services that J wants to run continuously.

At the time of this writing, this implements :
• Screensaver inhibiting service.
  There is an existing system service for this purpose
  under the org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver dbus service and
  in fact this daemon uses it. But this service tracks
  the processes inhibiting the screensaver and drops
  their lock when they terminate (which makes sense),
  meaning the process must stay running if the screen
  saver is to stay inhibited. That means you can't just
  just run a qdbus command to inhibit the screensaver
  because as soon as qdbus terminates the lock is
  released.
  So jdaemon supplies the long-running process. It
  exposes a ScreenSaver service under org.j.Daemon
  which mimics org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver, with the
  addition of a status and a toggle commands. Of course
  that means the lock held by jdaemon is cooperative,
  which would not be fine for the standard system
  service, but is fine for my own uses.
  With this, a simple qdbus command can be used to
  inhibit, uninhibit, or toggle the screensaver. It
  works conjointly with other locks (as in, if some
  other party is holding a lock, giving jdaemon the
  uninhibit command will not release it and the
  screensaver stays inhibited until the other party
  releases it, which is what we want).
  To use this service, the most obvious thing to do is
  to bind a key to
  qdbus org.j.Daemon /org/j/Daemon/ScreenSaver org.j.Daemon.ScreenSaver.ToggleInhibit
  ...or a script doing that. The command conveniently
  returns the current status after it ran, as a string,
  to display to the user.
  
This is meant to be a container to possibly implement
other services in the future. One option could be the
regular screenshotting service.

